---
// The Eater Map Section: the Rail, a plain masthead and its two authored lines,
// the copy, the four numbered points, and the Exploded View — the captured Slab
// tilted under one camera with the app's own three surfaces raised off it.
// NOTES.md carries the reasoning: why the masthead is a plain word rather than a
// Cut Title, which of the Kernel's lengths this Section reads and why that is
// not joining the landing measure, and the two pieces of arithmetic the stage
// turns on.
//
// THE MARKUP RESTS IN THE RAISED STATE, and that inverts the obvious build
// (#177). The Lift animates FROM flat TOWARDS raised, so the finished Exploded
// View is what a reader whose scripts never arrived and a reader who asked for
// reduced motion each get for nothing, and nothing here is contingent on a
// script. Flat is `--eater-map-lift: 0` and it is where the motion begins.
import { CARDS } from './cards';
import { content } from './content';
import { SLAB_PART } from './leaders';
import { SLAB } from './slab';
// The Eater app's own stylesheet, vendored with the Cards (#174). A plain CSS
// file, so Astro does not scope it — every selector in it begins with the
// `.eater-cards` host, and that host is the containment. NOTES.md.
import './assets/cards/cards.css';
import './tokens.css';
// variants.css is deliberately NOT imported: an unselected Variant has to cost
// the shipped page nothing, so the sheet is not part of the build at all and the
// render tool injects it. NOTES.md, and check-source.mjs if this is ever undone.
---

{/* data-section is the loader's handle: it names the Section whose timeline.ts
    is fetched as this element approaches the viewport, and it is what makes this
    a resting place — src/kernel/landing.css declares every Section after the
    first a port, as a relationship rather than a list, so nothing in the Kernel
    had to learn this Section's name.

    No data-turn. The crossing spans the FIRST Section and is finished by the
    time the Projects Panel comes to rest, so the page is already dark when the
    reader arrives here and there is no second crossing to run. src/kernel/turn.ts. */}
<section
  class="eater-map"
  id="eater-map"
  data-section="eater-map"
  aria-labelledby="eater-map-masthead"
>
  <nav class="eater-map__rail" aria-label={content.rail.label}>
    {/* role="list" on a list that already is one: `list-style: none` costs a
        list its semantics in VoiceOver, and the Rail is meant to be announced
        as a list of projects. */}
    <ul class="eater-map__rail-items" role="list">
      {content.rail.projects.map((project) => (
        <li
          class:list={[
            'eater-map__rail-item',
            { 'is-selected': project.href === '#eater-map' },
          ]}
        >
          {project.href ? (
            <a href={project.href} aria-current={project.href === '#eater-map' ? 'true' : undefined}>
              {project.name}
            </a>
          ) : (
            <Fragment>
              {project.name}
              <span class="eater-map__quiet">{content.rail.unbuilt}</span>
            </Fragment>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>
  <div class="eater-map__inner">
    <header class="eater-map__head">
      <h2 class="eater-map__masthead" id="eater-map-masthead">{content.masthead}</h2>
      <p class="eater-map__sub">
        {content.subheading.map((line) => (
          <span>{line}</span>
        ))}
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="eater-map__copy">
      {content.copy.map((paragraph) => (
        <p>{paragraph}</p>
      ))}
    </div>
    {/* THE EXPLODED VIEW: one still of the map, and the app's own three surfaces
        drawn on it at the scale the map is drawn at, so that flat it is
        indistinguishable from a screenshot of the phone — and is made of a
        picture and real text rather than of pixels. Raised, the same picture and
        the same three surfaces come apart under one camera.

        The two numbers below are the ONLY thing the markup says about geometry,
        they are not chosen, and they are not repeated anywhere: they are the
        capture's own, out of slab.ts, and every other length on this plane is
        derived from them or is a Token. A re-capture at another phone size moves
        them and the composition follows. NOTES.md.

        FOUR BOXES AND EACH HAS ONE JOB, which is one more than the flat state
        needed and is forced rather than chosen — NOTES.md has the two rules that
        force it. The Slab is the SIZE and the container the plane's lengths are
        shares of; the plane is the CAMERA and the one rotation everything on it
        shares; the Cards' box is the app's own stylesheet host; and each Card is
        a depth off the plane's surface. */}
    <div class="eater-map__stage">
      <div
        class="eater-map__slab"
        data-eater-map-watch
        data-eater-map-slab
        style={`--eater-map-app-w: ${SLAB.viewport.width}px; --eater-map-slab-ratio: ${SLAB.pixels.width} / ${SLAB.pixels.height}`}
      >
        <div class="eater-map__plane" data-eater-map-plane>
          {/* `lazy`, so the Slab's quarter of a megabyte arrives as the reader
              comes down the page rather than at load — the same promise the
              Section's own chunk makes, kept declaratively so it holds with no
              script at all. `alt` is Content: the picture carries a claim about
              the project and a reader who cannot see it is owed the claim. */}
          <img
            class="eater-map__still"
            data-eater-map-still
            src={SLAB.src}
            width={SLAB.pixels.width}
            height={SLAB.pixels.height}
            alt={content.stage.slabAlt}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          {/* `.eater-cards` is the vendored stylesheet's host and therefore its
              containment: every rule in that file begins with this class, so it
              reaches nothing above this element and nothing above it reaches in. */}
          <div class="eater-map__cards eater-cards">
            {CARDS.map((card) => (
              <div class="eater-map__card" data-eater-map-card={card.name}>
                {/* `display: contents`, so the wrapper this Card's own markup has
                    to arrive in — set:html replaces an element's children, and
                    the anchor beside it would be one — generates no box at all
                    and the Card is laid out exactly as it was without it. */}
                <div class="eater-map__surface" set:html={card.html} />
                <span class="eater-map__anchor" data-eater-map-anchor={card.name} />
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          {/* THE FOURTH ANCHOR IS ON THE PLANE ITSELF, because the fourth point
              names the Slab: the offline basemap is the artefact the reader is
              already looking at rather than something standing on it. It is a
              sibling of the picture and inside the same camera, so it is turned
              by the same one rotation everything else on the plane is. */}
          <span class="eater-map__anchor" data-eater-map-anchor={SLAB_PART} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    {/* An <ol> because each point is numbered on the page, and each names one
        part of the Exploded View — `point.part`, which the schema holds to
        exactly one point per part and exactly one part per point (#178).

        AFTER THE STAGE IN THE MARKUP AND NOT BEFORE IT, which is #179's doing and
        costs the band nothing: both boxes are placed by an explicit `grid-area`
        up there, so the drawing is left of the points whichever order they are
        written in. What the order buys is the COLLAPSE, where the column is
        source order and the four features have to read as a list BENEATH the
        Slab. Done with `order` or a `grid-row` instead, the reader looking at the
        page and the reader hearing it would be given two different sequences;
        moved here, there is one sequence in both regimes and in the document.

        `data-eater-map-watch` is the leader lines' redraw: this column changes
        height when a face arrives or a title wraps, and the Section's own box
        does not move when it does.

        THE HOOK IS WHERE A RULE LEAVES THIS ROW, and it is a BOX rather than a
        coordinate: its width is --eater-map-leader-reach, so the shoulder's two
        ends are its two edges and the script reads them off one rect instead of
        parsing a length out of a custom property, which computes to its own
        token stream rather than to pixels. leaders.ts. */}
    <ol class="eater-map__points" data-eater-map-watch>
      {content.points.map((point) => (
        <li>
          <h3>{point.title}</h3>
          <p>{point.figure}</p>
          <span class="eater-map__hook" data-eater-map-hook={point.part} aria-hidden="true" />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    {/* THE LEADER LINES. One rule per point, drawn by leaders.ts from the point's
        hook to a zero-sized anchor at the part's own corner — which is inside
        the camera, so what the rule is given is where that corner LANDED rather
        than where the Card's untransformed box is. #178.

        Last in the composition and positioned, so it paints over the drawing
        without a z-index. `aria-hidden`, because the rules carry nothing that is
        not already in the four points' own words: they are how a reader LOOKING
        at the page knows which claim belongs to which piece, and a reader
        listening is given the same correspondence by reading the points in
        order. Empty of text for the same reason. `pointer-events: none` in the
        stylesheet, so the overlay cannot take a selection off the words under it.

        The polylines carry no `points` until a script arrives, so a reader whose
        scripts never came gets the Exploded View with no rules over it — which
        is the one thing the Lift's inversion cannot buy, and costs nothing that
        is not also in the text. NOTES.md. */}
    <svg class="eater-map__leaders" data-eater-map-leaders aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      {content.points.map((point) => (
        <polyline data-eater-map-leader={point.part} />
      ))}
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Scoped by the build. Astro rewrites every selector below with this
     component's own attribute, so none of them can match an element in another
     Section even by accident.

     THE SECTION PAINTS NO GROUND, and that is the rule rather than an omission.
     The page's ground is `html { background: var(--ground) }` in the Kernel and
     --ground is itself a crossing on --turn, so a Section that paints its own
     composes two crossings and puts a step across the page at its own top edge —
     which is what "banding" has meant both times it was reported. This Section
     inherits the page's ground and its ink in both themes, at the far end of a
     Turn that finished before the reader got here. */
  .eater-map {
    /* THE LIFT'S PLAYHEAD, AND IT IS NOT A TOKEN. 0 is the flat screenshot and 1
       is the finished Exploded View; the tilt, the depths and the gaps below are
       all it times a Token. It is declared HERE rather than in tokens.css
       because it is not a number the author chooses — `timeline.ts` writes it,
       the Editor's Timeline scrub is the control for it, and a second control in
       the Tokens panel would be two handles on one thing.

       IT RESTS AT 1, which is the whole of the inversion #177 asks for: the
       markup reads as the finished composition, the Timeline animates AWAY from
       it, and a reader whose scripts never arrived keeps the Exploded View
       rather than a half-built one. */
    --eater-map-lift: 1;

    /* IS THE EXPLODED VIEW DRAWN HERE? 0 where it is, 1 where the composition has
       collapsed to a flat full-bleed Slab with the four features under it (#179).

       IT IS NOT A TOKEN AND IT IS NOT A SECOND COPY OF THE BREAKPOINT — it is the
       breakpoint's ANSWER, declared where the breakpoint is. `timeline.ts` has to
       know whether there is anything to lift, and the two ways of telling it are
       a `matchMedia('(min-width: 1100px)')` in the module, which is the number
       written twice and drifting the day one of them moves, or this, which is the
       stylesheet saying what it already decided. A Token could not carry it at
       all: `check-source.mjs` refuses an `@media` block in tokens.css, and rightly
       — the Editor writes a value, not a breakpoint (src/sections/NOTES.md). */
    --eater-map-collapsed: 0;
    /* WHETHER THE SLAB IS A SOLID, which is the same kind of declaration as the
       two above it and is read by the stage rather than by this stylesheet. 1 in
       the band, 0 in the collapse — see there. */
    --eater-map-solid: 1;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    display: grid;
    /* The Rail's column IS the page's left margin, the way it is in the Projects
       Panel: the names are set across it, so the margin has a floor under it and
       that floor is one rotated line of --eater-map-rail-size at its leading. */
    grid-template-columns: var(--eater-map-side) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--eater-map-gap);
    /* STRETCH AND NOT CENTRE, and it is the difference between a composition
       that fits the screen and one that is taller than it. The Rail is spread
       `space-between` down its whole column and the stage is row two's
       remainder, so both need the Section's own height handed to them — centring
       would size the box to its content instead, and the content would then be
       whatever the four points and a picture-shaped box happen to add up to. */
    align-items: stretch;
    min-height: var(--fold);
    padding-block: var(--eater-map-inset);
    padding-inline-end: var(--eater-map-side);
    /* Clip, not hidden, and only across: `hidden` would make the Section a
       scroll container and break the Rail. */
    overflow-x: clip;
    font-family: var(--face-panel);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--eater-map-leading);
  }

  /* ---- the Rail ---------------------------------------------------------- */
  .eater-map__rail {
    display: flex;
    align-self: stretch;
  }

  .eater-map__rail-items {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .eater-map__rail-item {
    /* Turned per ITEM and not on the list: rotating the list would rotate the
       order with it and put the last project at the top. */
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    transform: rotate(180deg);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-rail-size);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-rail-tracking);
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-muted-veil));
  }

  .eater-map__rail-item.is-selected {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .eater-map__rail-item a {
    color: inherit;
    /* Three words in a margin: an underline across a rotated one reads as a
       strikethrough. */
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .eater-map__rail-item:not(.is-selected) a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .eater-map__rail-item a:focus-visible {
    outline: 1px solid currentColor;
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .eater-map__quiet {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ---- the composition: twelve columns, two rows ------------------------- */
  .eater-map__inner {
    /* The leader lines' overlay is absolute against THIS box, which is what
       makes every coordinate in leaders.ts independent of the scroll — a rule
       computed against the viewport would be dragged across the composition by
       the page turn. `relative` with no z-index is not a stacking context, so
       the Exploded View still sorts its own three dimensions. */
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    /* THE HEAD IS ITS OWN SIZE AND THE PICTURE IS EVERYTHING LEFT. `minmax(0,
       1fr)` and not `1fr`: a bare `1fr` floors at the row's min-content, so the
       four points and the box would push the Section past the fold rather than
       being given what the screen has. NOTES.md. */
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--eater-map-gutter);
    /* The one seam this box owns is the break between the writing and the
       picture, which is the biggest step on the ladder. */
    row-gap: var(--eater-map-act);
  }

  .eater-map__head {
    grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 7;
    align-self: end;
  }

  /* ---- how the capitalised things are set --------------------------------
     Listed rather than hoisted onto the Section: `case` on the copy would raise
     the hyphen in "offline-first" off its own baseline, and the figures are
     sentences rather than labels. */
  .eater-map__masthead,
  .eater-map__sub,
  .eater-map__rail-item,
  /* Each row is the containing block for its own hook, so `left: 100%` is this
     column's right edge rather than the page's. */
  .eater-map__points li {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* WHERE A RULE LEAVES ITS POINT, and how far it runs before it turns. A box
     rather than two numbers: its width is the reach Token spent on a real
     property, so leaders.ts reads the shoulder's two ends off one rect instead
     of parsing `0.9rem` out of a custom property, which is all a custom
     property's computed value ever is.

     Half a line of the title down from the row's top, so a rule leaves the
     TITLE rather than the middle of a row whose height is however long its
     figure ran. Derived and not a Token: it is a coordinate in the composition
     rather than a number the author chooses (ADR 0004). */
  .eater-map__hook {
    position: absolute;
    left: 100%;
    top: calc(var(--eater-map-point-size) * var(--eater-map-point-leading) / 2);
    width: var(--eater-map-leader-reach);
    height: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .eater-map__points h3 {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-feature-settings: 'case' 1;
  }

  /* A PLAIN MASTHEAD, AND IT PAINTS. The Projects Panel's is hidden because the
     Front Screen's Cut Title is already that Section's head; nothing is cut into
     this one's slot, because #172 measured what a second landing solve would
     cost and the answer was the Panel's whole marble corner. NOTES.md. */
  .eater-map__masthead {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-masthead-size);
    font-weight: var(--eater-map-masthead-weight);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-masthead-leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-masthead-tracking);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .eater-map__sub {
    margin: var(--eater-map-close) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-sub-size);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--eater-map-sub-leading);
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-muted-veil));
  }

  /* `block` on the spans rather than a <br>, so the break is a property of the
     composition and not a character a screen reader has to read around. */
  .eater-map__sub span {
    display: block;
  }

  .eater-map__copy {
    grid-area: 1 / 7 / 2 / 13;
    align-self: end;
    border-left: 1px solid
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-rule-veil));
    padding-left: var(--eater-map-copy-inset);
    font-size: var(--eater-map-copy-size);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-copy-leading);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .eater-map__copy p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .eater-map__copy p + p {
    margin-top: 1em;
  }

  /* The four points take the whole of their column and spread themselves down
     it, the way the Panel's do: the air is what a point growing a paragraph
     spends, down to the step and no further. */
  .eater-map__points {
    grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 4;
    align-self: stretch;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-rows: min-content;
    align-content: space-between;
    row-gap: var(--eater-map-step);
  }

  .eater-map__points h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-point-size);
    font-weight: var(--eater-map-point-weight);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-point-leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-point-tracking);
    color: var(--ink);
    /* A title set to fit its column on one line at the window it was drawn at
       wraps on a narrower one rather than being made to fit by a size nobody
       chose; `balance` makes the two lines halves of a title. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .eater-map__points p {
    margin: var(--eater-map-figure-air) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-figure-size);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-figure-leading);
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-muted-veil));
  }

  /* ---- the Exploded View --------------------------------------------------
     A picture of the map, and the app's own three surfaces on it. What makes it
     read as one screenshot rather than as three stickers on a photograph is that
     the whole plane is drawn in ONE coordinate system — the phone's — and
     everything on it is a share of that. What keeps that true once it comes
     apart is that there is ONE camera and ONE rotation, both on the plane, and
     every part of the drawing is inside it. NOTES.md. */
  .eater-map__stage {
    grid-area: 2 / 4 / 3 / 13;
    align-self: stretch;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* THE PLANE. Its height is what row two has left, times the one Token that
     says how much of that to take, and its width follows from the capture's own
     proportion — so the phone is as big as the screen allows at every window in
     the band and the composition never has to be told a size.

     `container-type: inline-size` is what lets everything on the plane be a
     share of the plane. It is the Frame's arrangement in the Projects Panel and
     it carries the Frame's trap with it: AN ELEMENT IS NOT ITS OWN CONTAINER, so
     a `cqw` written on this rule would silently resolve against the viewport.
     Every one of them below is on a descendant.

     NOT CLIPPED, AND IT USED TO BE. While the Cards lay flat on the picture a
     Card hanging off its edge was a Card not lying on it, and `overflow: hidden`
     said so. Raised, a Card standing off the surface is exactly what the drawing
     is of — and the clip could not have stayed in any case: `overflow` other
     than `visible` makes an element a GROUPING element, and a grouping element's
     `transform-style: preserve-3d` is forced back to `flat`, so the clip alone
     would have flattened the Cards into the picture with no error and no
     warning. NOTES.md. */
  .eater-map__slab {
    position: relative;
    height: calc(var(--eater-map-slab-fill) * 100%);
    aspect-ratio: var(--eater-map-slab-ratio);
    /* A percentage translate resolves against the element's own box, so this is
       a share of the phone rather than of whatever the stage happens to be. */
    translate: calc(var(--eater-map-slab-shift) * 100%);
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* THE ONE ROTATION EVERY PART SHARES, AND IT NEVER MOVES. The picture and all
     three Cards are inside this box, so there is exactly one projection on the
     page and no element carries a rotation of its own — which is what makes this
     one object taken apart rather than three stickers on a photograph.

     NOT A TERM OF THE LIFT, WHICH IS #189. Both angles used to be multiplied by
     `--eater-map-lift`, so the Slab tilted itself up as the reader arrived and
     progress 0 was the flat screenshot #176 built. It stands at its attitude the
     whole time now: the same solid, at the same size, with the same projected
     corners at both ends of the Lift and at both ends of the band, and only the
     Cards move. The Lift's flat end is the Cards down on a plane that is already
     turned, and the flat screenshot is no longer a state the drawing passes
     through.

     A PARALLEL PROJECTION, WHICH IS THE ABSENCE OF `perspective()` and not a
     camera pushed far away — a large camera only flattens towards this and never
     reaches it, which is why #180's isometric Variant had to restate this whole
     list. So there is no `perspective()`, no `--eater-map-camera` and no dolly:
     nothing converges, so nothing grows as it climbs and there is nothing for a
     camera pulling back to pay for. `preserve-3d` still carries each Card's
     depth, and with no convergence that reads as an offset along the plane's own
     normal — which is how an exploded view is drawn on paper.

     `preserve-3d` HERE AND NOT ON THE SLAB. The Slab is `container-type`, which
     is `contain: layout`, which is grouping, and a grouping element's
     `preserve-3d` is forced back to `flat` with no error and no warning — the
     Cards would lie flat on the map for ever, at the right scale, looking like a
     screenshot. NOTES.md carries that rule and the one under it. */
  .eater-map__plane {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
    transform: rotateX(var(--eater-map-tilt)) rotateZ(var(--eater-map-swing));
  }

  .eater-map__still {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* THE ONE PIECE OF ARITHMETIC THE STAGE TURNS ON: the scale everything on the
     plane is drawn at is the Slab's drawn width over the phone Eater was driven
     at. Get it from anywhere else — a number chosen by eye, a share of the
     viewport — and the Cards are the right size at one window and floating over
     a map of the wrong scale at every other.

     `tan(atan2(a, b))` is `a / b`. CSS has no other way to divide one length by
     another: `calc()` requires the right-hand side of a `/` to be a number, and
     that is the spec rather than an engine's gap. NOTES.md.

     `preserve-3d` here is not decoration either: without it this box flattens its
     children into itself and every Card's depth is discarded silently — the Cards
     would still be there, at the right scale, lying on the map for ever. */
  .eater-map__cards {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
    --eater-map-app-scale: tan(atan2(100cqw, var(--eater-map-app-w)));
    /* The app's own three glass colours, taken off its host so the Cards can be
       given something to stand on once the Lift takes their backdrop away. Read
       here and spent one element down, because a custom property that refers to
       its own name is a cycle and computes to nothing. See below. */
    --eater-map-glass: var(--glass);
    --eater-map-glass-sheet: var(--glass-sheet);
    --eater-map-glass-float: var(--glass-sheet-float);
  }

  /* An engine without the trigonometric functions gets a plausible screenshot
     rather than an interface drawn at the app's own pixel size over a map a
     third that scale, which is what an invalid scale would leave. The number is
     the ratio at the window the Checks run at. */
  @supports not (scale: tan(atan2(1px, 1px))) {
    .eater-map__cards {
      --eater-map-app-scale: 0.72;
    }
  }

  /* Each Card at its own corner of the picture, in the picture's own units, and
     at its own depth off it. The Cards carry their own frozen pixel sizes — they
     are the app's markup at the window it was exported at (NOTES.md) — so the
     scale is a transform and never a width: a width would reflow another
     repository's interface.

     THE CARD'S OWN PLAYHEAD, defaulting to the Section's. `timeline.ts` writes
     this one per Card so the three can climb one after another; nothing writes
     it when no script arrives, and the `var()` then hands each Card the Section's
     own 1 and the stack is up. Both are the same number and neither is a Token.

     TRANSLATE FIRST AND SCALE SECOND, and the order is the arithmetic: a
     transform list applies right to left, so the scale is the Card's own and the
     translate is in the PLANE's units — a depth in the picture's own coordinate
     system rather than in the app's. Written the other way round the app's own
     scale would multiply the depth and each Card would rise by a different
     amount for the same Token. All three distances are `cqw`, so they are shares
     of the Slab's WIDTH as everything else on the plane is — including the `top`
     below, which is why it is not a percentage (#189): a percentage `top`
     resolves against the containing block's HEIGHT, which put the placement and
     the slide in two different units while they read as a pair.

     GLASS CANNOT BE CARRIED OFF A SURFACE, and this is the one thing the Lift
     costs that no Token can put back. A `backdrop-filter` samples what is painted
     behind an element IN ITS OWN PLANE, and there is no such thing once the plane
     is turned under a camera: Chromium hands the filter an empty backdrop and it
     becomes a no-op, silently, at the FIRST DEGREE of tilt. Measured — with the
     depths and the gap set to 0 and only the tilt standing, the app's frosted
     detail panel is already a sheet of clear glass with a sharp map behind its
     text, which is a composition a reader cannot read. NOTES.md.

     So the composition gives each Card the plate its glass was standing in for,
     in step with the Card's own climb: on the Slab the app's own translucency is
     what a screenshot has, and off it the same surfaces are filled to
     --eater-map-plate. The three values are the APP'S OWN, mixed rather than
     restated — this Section never says what colour Eater's glass is, only which
     way it fills as it leaves the map. */
  .eater-map__card {
    --eater-map-card-lift: var(--eater-map-lift);
    --eater-map-card-rise: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-rise) * var(--eater-map-card-depth)
    );
    /* WHERE THE CARD SLIDES TO as it climbs, along the plane, in both of the
       plane's own directions and as a share of the Slab's width. Two Tokens per
       Card rather than one gap spent by the depth (#189): the three depths are
       within 12% of each other now, so `(0.5 - depth)` would slide all three the
       same way by the same amount, which is a translation of the drawing rather
       than a gap in it.

       BOTH FALL BACK TO 0, and that is not belt and braces. A `var()` with no
       fallback makes the whole `transform` below invalid the moment a Card has no
       per-name block — so a fourth Card added to cards.ts without one would be
       drawn UNROTATED at the app's own frozen pixel size over a map at 0.72 of
       it, which reads as a broken vendoring rather than as a missing Token. 0 is
       a Card that does not slide, which is the honest reading. */
    --eater-map-card-drift-x: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-card-slide-x, 0)
    );
    --eater-map-card-drift-y: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-card-slide-y, 0)
    );
    --eater-map-card-fill: calc(var(--eater-map-card-lift) * 100%);
    --glass: color-mix(in srgb, var(--eater-map-glass), var(--eater-map-plate) var(--eater-map-card-fill));
    --glass-sheet: color-mix(
      in srgb,
      var(--eater-map-glass-sheet),
      var(--eater-map-plate) var(--eater-map-card-fill)
    );
    --glass-sheet-float: color-mix(
      in srgb,
      var(--eater-map-glass-float),
      var(--eater-map-plate) var(--eater-map-card-fill)
    );
    position: absolute;
    left: calc(var(--eater-map-card-x) * 100cqw);
    top: calc(var(--eater-map-card-y) * 100cqw);
    transform-origin: 0 0;
    transform: translate3d(
        calc(var(--eater-map-card-drift-x) * 100cqw),
        calc(var(--eater-map-card-drift-y) * 100cqw),
        calc(var(--eater-map-card-rise) * 100cqw)
      )
      scale(calc(var(--eater-map-app-scale) * var(--eater-map-card-scale)));
  }

  /* The wrapper `set:html` needs and the layout does not: `contents` generates
     no box, so the Card is laid out exactly as it was when its own markup was
     the Card's only child. cards.css has no child combinator in it — checked —
     so nothing in the vendored stylesheet notices either way. */
  .eater-map__surface {
    display: contents;
  }

  /* WHERE A LEADER LINE ENDS, and the whole reason the anchors are elements
     rather than arithmetic. This box is ZERO-SIZED and lives INSIDE the camera,
     so it projects to a POINT and `getBoundingClientRect()` hands back that
     corner's actual position on screen. Reading the Card's own rect instead
     gives the axis-aligned bounding box of the projected quad, whose corners are
     nowhere on the Card once it is turned — which looks right at the flat end of
     the Lift and wrong at every other frame of it. leaders.ts.

     A share of the part it is on, so it is a corner at every window and at every
     size the Slab is drawn at, and so dragging it in the Editor moves it around
     the Card rather than around the page. */
  .eater-map__anchor {
    position: absolute;
    left: calc(var(--eater-map-anchor-x) * 100%);
    top: calc(var(--eater-map-anchor-y) * 100%);
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='search'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-search-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-search-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='lines'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-lines-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-lines-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='details'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-details-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-details-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='slab'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-slab-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-slab-y);
  }

  /* ---- the leader lines --------------------------------------------------
     Four thin rules over the composition, joining each numbered point to the
     part of the Exploded View it names. The geometry is leaders.ts's — nothing
     here says where a rule goes, because where a projected corner LANDED is a
     fact only the compositor has.

     `overflow: visible`, because a Card raised off the Slab can stand outside
     the composition's own box and the rule to it has to follow. The Section's
     own `overflow-x: clip` is still what stops anything leaving the page.

     The rules are painted from --ink like every other quiet mark in this Section
     — mixed towards transparent rather than towards a colour, so they sit
     correctly in both themes at the far end of a Turn that finished before the
     reader arrived. */
  .eater-map__leaders {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    overflow: visible;
    pointer-events: none;
    fill: none;
    stroke: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-leader-veil));
    stroke-width: var(--eater-map-leader-weight);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='search'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-search-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-search-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-search-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-search-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-search-slide-y);
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='lines'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-lines-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-lines-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-lines-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-lines-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-lines-slide-y);
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='details'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-details-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-details-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-details-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-details-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-details-slide-y);
  }

  /* ---- one column, outside the band the composition is fitted to ---------
     The same move the Projects Panel makes at the same width: the two-column
     drawing stops earning its columns, so it becomes a stack as tall as it needs
     to be, with the Rail a row across the top instead of an index down an edge.

     AND THE EXPLODED VIEW COLLAPSES WITH IT (#179). An Exploded View is a drawing
     fitted to a wide window: it spends width on a camera, on the distance the
     pieces draw apart along the plane, and on a stage that is a row's remainder,
     and a column has none of those to give. So out here the Slab lies flat and
     runs to both edges of the window, and the four features are an ordinary list
     under it.

     ONE COMPOSITION AND NOT THREE. It is written as rules on the MARKUP'S OWN
     RESTING STATE rather than as anything a script does, which is what makes the
     narrow reader, the reader who asked for no motion and the reader whose
     scripts never arrived all get the same drawing — one thing to build, and one
     equality for the Check to assert rather than three separate compositions.
     `timeline.ts` reads --eater-map-collapsed and never runs the Lift down here,
     so the flat state is where the drawing STAYS rather than where it starts. */
  @media not all and (min-width: 1100px) {
    .eater-map {
      --eater-map-collapsed: 1;
      /* FLAT, AND SAID HERE AS WELL AS ON THE PLANE BELOW. `transform: none`
         answers for the geometry; this answers for everything else the playhead
         is spent on — the Cards' glass, which fills towards --eater-map-plate as
         a Card leaves the map and must not be filled for a Card that never left
         it. A screenshot has the app's own translucency. */
      --eater-map-lift: 0;
      /* AND NO THICKNESS, for the same reason and by the same route. The Slab's
         depth stopped being a term of the playhead in #189, so `--eater-map-lift:
         0` no longer takes it away — and an edge is a thing an Exploded View has,
         not a thing a full-bleed picture at the top of a column has. The stage
         multiplies its depth and its radius by this, so the whole solid closes up
         to the flat picture down here and opens again on a resize back into the
         band without anything being re-mounted. Declared beside the playhead and
         not in tokens.css for the reason --eater-map-collapsed is: it is a regime
         and not a number the author chooses. */
      --eater-map-solid: 0;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      row-gap: var(--eater-map-move);
      min-height: 0;
      padding-inline: var(--eater-map-side);
    }

    /* Three names across the top and they wrap: 360 and 320 are real screens and
       the longest of the three does not fit beside the other two on either. Both
       gaps are multiples of the names' own size, so the row shrinks as one
       drawing. */
    .eater-map__rail-items {
      flex-direction: row;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      justify-content: flex-start;
      align-items: baseline;
      column-gap: calc(var(--eater-map-rail-gap) * var(--eater-map-rail-size));
      row-gap: calc(var(--eater-map-rail-rows) * var(--eater-map-rail-size));
    }

    .eater-map__rail-item {
      writing-mode: horizontal-tb;
      transform: none;
    }

    /* NO ROW GAP, AND THE SEAMS ARE MARGINS INSTEAD. A grid's row gap is one
       length for every row it has, and the seams down this column are different
       sizes of decision — the head to the copy is not the copy to the picture. */
    .eater-map__inner {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-rows: none;
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    /* `grid-row: auto` AND NOT ONLY `grid-column: 1`, which is the trap this
       reset has: the four areas above are `grid-area` shorthands, so each pins a
       ROW as well as a column. Taking the columns back and leaving the rows
       stacks the head on the copy and the points inside the picture — two pairs
       of overlapping text, which is what it did. Auto-placed, they come out in
       source order, which is the order this column reads in. */
    .eater-map__head,
    .eater-map__copy,
    .eater-map__points,
    .eater-map__stage {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: auto;
      align-self: auto;
    }

    /* The rule moves from the paragraph's left edge to its top, which is where a
       divider goes when the thing it divides is above rather than beside. */
    .eater-map__copy {
      border-left: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-rule-veil));
      padding-left: 0;
      padding-top: var(--eater-map-close);
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-move);
    }

    .eater-map__points {
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-act);
      row-gap: var(--eater-map-move);
    }

    /* FULL-BLEED, AND THE NEGATIVE MARGIN IS WHY IT IS NOT `100vw`. The Section
       carries the page's margin on both edges out here, and a picture that runs
       to the window's edges has to spend both of them back. `100vw` would be the
       obvious way and is wrong by exactly the scrollbar's gutter on every
       platform that reserves one — the trap `projects-panel` paid for and
       scripts/checks/NOTES.md records, and it is worse here than it was there,
       because this box is CENTRED in what it overflows and would hang the error
       half out of each edge. A margin is stated against the box's own containing
       block, so it lands on the window whether or not there is a gutter. */
    .eater-map__stage {
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-act);
      margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--eater-map-side));
    }

    /* NO LEADER LINES OUT HERE, and absent rather than redrawn (#178). The
       composition is one column with the four points BENEATH the picture, so a
       rule from a point to its part would run back up the page and join a
       paragraph to a corner off the top of the screen. They may simply go,
       because everything they carry is in the points' own words — and they have
       to, because a rule drawn before a resize would otherwise still be lying
       across the stack afterwards. leaders.ts asks this declaration rather than
       repeating the breakpoint, so the collapse is decided in one place. */
    .eater-map__leaders {
      display: none;
    }

    /* THE SLAB IS THE WHOLE OF THAT, and the proportion carries the height, which
       is the same way round as the band reads it — there the height is the row's
       remainder and the width follows, here the width is the window and the
       height follows. What went with it is `--eater-map-slab-cap`: a ceiling on
       the picture's width was the interim's, and full-bleed is a picture with no
       ceiling by definition (#179). And `translate` goes too — the shift slides
       the Slab across a stage it is narrower than, and out here it is the stage. */
    .eater-map__slab {
      height: auto;
      width: 100%;
      translate: none;
    }

    /* NO PART OF THE PROJECTION SURVIVES, AND THIS IS NOW THE ONLY THING SAYING
       SO. It used to be belt and braces — every angle was a term of
       --eater-map-lift, so the 0 above already flattened the plane and this said
       it outright for the things that read the drawing rather than look at it.
       Since #189 the attitude is a constant the playhead does not touch, so
       without these two declarations a column below the band would carry a Slab
       tilted 52 degrees. `transform-style: flat` goes with it for the reason it
       always did: the Cards would otherwise still stand in a 3D rendering
       context, and the leader lines, a Variant and a Check would each be asking
       a flattened projection rather than a plane. */
    .eater-map__plane {
      transform: none;
      transform-style: flat;
    }

    .eater-map__cards {
      transform-style: flat;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the landing band --------------------------------------------------
     Two of the Kernel's lengths and no more: the page's top margin and its left
     one, so this screen's white agrees with the Panel's and both Rails stand in
     one column. NOT the landing MEASURE — --landing-w, --landing-cap and the
     masthead's drop are the Cut Title's, and #172 settled that this Section does
     not carry one. src/kernel/NOTES.md has the numbers that settled it. */
  @media (min-width: 1100px) and (min-height: 700px) {
    .eater-map {
      grid-template-columns: var(--landing-side) minmax(0, 1fr);
      padding-block: var(--landing-inset);
      padding-inline-end: var(--landing-side);
    }
  }
</style>
